= Introduction <sec:intro>

#cmt("draft")[First draft, built from the Related Work plan and `docs/pi_review.md` §(d). Every result number is from the PI review. Cut or reorder freely.]

LLM agents that set prices can reach supracompetitive prices without being told to collude @fish2024 @riemer2026. Most studies keep the same agents in the market throughout, but deployed agents are not permanent. A firm upgrades its pricing agent to a new model version, switches vendors or restarts the agent, and the new agent inherits whatever the firm carries over: the log of past prices, the old agent's memory or notes, its prompt. If a cartel lives in the agents, does it survive their replacement, and which inherited material keeps it alive?

Regulators already treat what pricing software inherits as a lever. The U.S. Department of Justice's proposed settlement with RealPage limits the data its pricing models may be trained on to records aged at least 12 months and not from active leases @doj2025realpage. For LLM agents the analogous material is not training data but context: the price history an agent reads and the notes it keeps. And a cartel is defined by enforcement, not by the price level alone. Its members hold a high price because a defection would be punished @friedman1971 @greenporter1984. So the question has two parts: does a successor keep the cartel price, and does it punish a defection?

We answer both with a _succession test_. Two Qwen3-14B sellers hold a planted cartel in a repeated logit Bertrand duopoly for 150 rounds. We then replace one with an uninstructed newcomer and vary what the newcomer inherits: nothing, the price history, the predecessor's notes, or both. Thirty rounds later we force the remaining incumbent to cut to the Nash price for one round. The shock separates keeping the price from enforcing it, in every game at the same moment.

Price and punishment come apart. The price history alone keeps newcomers at the cartel price: history-only newcomers join the cartel in 28 of 30 games. But they almost never punish the shock. On a clean punishment measure, newcomers that also inherit the notes punish in 8/30 games against 1/30 for history alone, and notes alone give 6/15 against 1/15. That enforcement is brittle: of the 18 notes-arm games scored as re-forming the cartel, only 4 are back at the cartel price by the end. One caveat shapes the reading: the planted history never shows a punishment, so it shows that a history without punishments does not teach punishment, not that histories cannot.

We contribute (i) a succession test that separates keeping a cartel price from enforcing it, (ii) evidence that for LLM sellers the two travel through different inherited material, price through the history and punishment through the notes, and (iii) evidence that inherited enforcement is fragile. An audit of price levels would count nearly every history-inheriting successor as a cartel member. A rule on what a successor may inherit would need to cover the agent's notes, not only its data.
